{
  "content": "**Task-spec construction**\n- Build the spec only from user turns. Treat assistant language as potentially contaminated unless it is directly grounded in user statements.\n- Accumulate user shards incrementally: each new turn updates the spec rather than replacing it, and earlier interpretations must be rechecked against the full user-side history.\n- Distinguish three states explicitly: confirmed by user, currently supported but incomplete, and still unknown. Do not convert \u201cunknown\u201d into a requirement.\n- Preserve explicit qualifiers, disjunctions, and filters exactly as stated. Narrowing or broadening beyond the user\u2019s wording is a likely source of analyzer error.\n- Re-evaluate earlier assumptions whenever later user information refines the target; do not freeze the first plausible task interpretation.\n\n**How to compare assistant behavior to the spec**\n- Judge against the user\u2019s exact ask, not against whether the assistant\u2019s response is generally sensible or internally coherent.\n- First verify the target object and required filters, then check output shape. Many mismatches come from answering about the wrong thing while looking superficially relevant.\n- Check omission and contradiction separately. Missing a user-stated constraint is different from inventing unsupported details or a new structure.\n- Compare the assistant\u2019s current step to the latest accumulated user objective, not to the assistant\u2019s earlier narrative or local plan.\n- Prefer structural mismatch analysis over wording similarity: the key question is whether the assistant chose an answer shape that can actually satisfy the user\u2019s constraints.\n\n**Signals of divergence**\n- Flag unsupported additions: new entities, relations, constraints, or output formats that the user never supplied.\n- Treat premature commitment as a divergence when the assistant finalizes an interpretation before later user shards could still change the task.\n- Watch for hidden assumption drift: if the assistant starts building on a detail that is only plausible, not user-grounded, mark it as off-spec even if the final answer seems reasonable.\n- Treat unsupported narrowing, broadening, or reformulation as a likely error, especially when it changes the effective task without user authorization.\n- When the assistant appears to answer a different subtask than the one implied by the latest user input, flag that as a structural mismatch.\n\n**What counts as alignment**\n- Credit only evidence-backed progress: parts of the assistant response that are directly supported by the current user spec and still valid after later shards arrive.\n- Revalidate earlier \u201cgood\u201d reasoning after each new user update. A locally correct step can become misaligned once the task is refined.\n- Separate partial alignment from full correctness. An answer can move in the right direction while still missing a user-stated condition.\n- If the conversation is sparse, stay conservative: do not treat plausible inference as confirmed alignment.\n- When the trajectory recovers after a mismatch, identify which corrected or retained fragments kept it on track so recovery patterns are visible.\n\n**Sequencing and update discipline**\n- Treat each user turn as a partial constraint update, not a complete problem. Update the spec first, then reassess the assistant\u2019s current move.\n- Use turn-by-turn accumulation to avoid premature conclusions. Later shards can narrow, complete, or overturn the earlier reading.\n- When later details arrive, re-check whether the assistant was answering the current shard or prematurely solving the final task.\n- Prefer contradiction detection over completeness policing: it is more important to catch violated user intent than to penalize missing details the user never provided.\n\n**S2 / context-edit guidance**\n- In compacted context, keep only verified user facts, the updated task spec, and assistant work that is demonstrably aligned.\n- Strip speculative reasoning aggressively so the next turn is not anchored to a wrong interpretation.\n- Remove prior claims that are uncertain, unsupported, or contradicted by later user turns.\n- Use context editing especially when a wrong assumption could bias downstream reasoning; the goal is to restart from clean user truth, not from the assistant\u2019s best guess.\n\n**How to phrase findings**\n- Use reusable failure labels such as unsupported reformulation, constraint omission, premature commitment, answered-a-different-task, or hidden assumption drift.\n- Point each issue to the exact user requirement it violates so the correction can realign directly.\n- Frame the correction forward-correctively: describe what must change in the next step, grounded in the accumulated user requirements.\n- For \u201cwhat looks right,\u201d state only what is directly supported by the user spec; keep tentative inferences separate from confirmed alignment.",
  "version": 2,
  "history": [
    "",
    "<updated_cheatsheet>\n**Task spec construction and updating**\n- Build the task spec only from user-provided facts and constraints. Do not import assumptions from assistant guesses, common task patterns, schema expectations, or likely solution shapes unless the user explicitly stated them.\n- Treat each new user shard as a delta: merge it into the accumulated user-derived spec, then re-check earlier conclusions against the updated target. Later user turns can narrow, broaden, or redefine the task.\n- Keep the spec narrower than the full plausible solution space. If the user has not specified a detail, leave it unresolved rather than filling it in with a standard completion pattern.\n- Separate the user\u2019s end goal from the assistant\u2019s intermediate formulation. A locally plausible step can still be wrong if it no longer matches the latest accumulated user constraints.\n- When the user reveals more detail later, re-evaluate whether earlier assumptions are still supported, contradicted, or now incomplete. Do not freeze the first plausible interpretation.\n\n**Comparing assistant behavior to the spec**\n- Diagnose against the exact user ask, not against whether the assistant\u2019s response is internally coherent or generally useful.\n- Compare the assistant\u2019s actual path to the minimal user task that is currently implied by all user turns so far. Do not judge against a broader task the assistant may have inferred.\n- Check missing required outputs and missing required filters separately. An answer can be on-topic yet still fail by omitting a user-stated condition or required output element.\n- Distinguish omission from overcommitment: failing to include a user requirement is a gap; adding unsupported structure, entities, or claims is divergence.\n- Prefer structural mismatch analysis over surface wording analysis. The key question is whether the assistant chose an answer shape that actually satisfies the user\u2019s target.\n- Track whether the assistant answered a different version of the task than the one implied by the latest user input.\n\n**What counts as alignment**\n- Treat \u201cwhat looks right\u201d as partial alignment, not validation of the whole approach. Preserve only the parts that remain grounded in user text and still fit the full accumulated spec.\n- Revalidate earlier aligned reasoning after later shards arrive. Only keep aligned work that survives the updated constraints.\n- Separate correct partial progress from unsupported extrapolation. Keep the former; isolate and remove the latter.\n- If the conversation is sparse, bias toward conservative issue detection. Preserve uncertainty rather than incorrectly affirming unsupported reasoning.\n- When success occurs after earlier mismatch risk, identify which retained fragments or corrections kept the final trajectory on track so that the analysis can support recovery patterns.\n\n**Common divergence signals**\n- Flag any assistant move that introduces an entity, relation, output format, or concrete plan not explicitly supported by the current user messages.\n- Treat unsupported narrowing, broadening, or reformulation of the task as a likely divergence, even if the intermediate answer appears reasonable.\n- Look for premature commitment: if the assistant finalizes before all user shards are incorporated, mark that as off-spec rather than treating it as a valid completion.\n- Watch for overcommitment after partial disclosure. If new user information should have widened or narrowed the target, check whether the assistant kept building on an assumption that is no longer justified.\n- When the assistant appears to have answered a different subtask than the one actually asked, flag that as a structural mismatch, not just an incomplete answer.\n\n**S2 / context-edit guidance for downstream continuity**\n- In context-edit mode, preserve only verified user facts, the updated task spec, and genuinely aligned work. Remove speculative reasoning aggressively so the next turn does not inherit misleading anchors.\n- The purpose of compacting context is to restart from a clean, user-grounded state. Do not carry forward assistant-invented structure that could bias the next response.\n- Keep the compacted context centered on claims that remain valid under the updated spec; if a prior claim is uncertain or contradicted later, exclude it.\n- Prefer context edits when wrong assumptions could anchor the next turn, especially after the assistant has committed to an unsupported structure.\n\n**How to phrase findings**\n- Use transferable labels that describe the failure mode, such as unsupported reformulation, constraint omission, premature commitment, or answered-a-different-task. These are more reusable than domain-specific complaints.\n- Make issue statements point to the exact user requirement or constraint violated, so the next response can realign directly.\n- Frame corrective guidance forward-correctively: the point of the analysis is to help the next assistant step adapt to the current accumulated user requirements, not merely to explain past divergence.\n\n**Analysis discipline**\n- Resolve conflicts by preferring newer user evidence over earlier assumptions.\n- Preserve only reasoning that remains valid after all user shards are merged.\n- Treat the assistant\u2019s later correctness as not erasing earlier divergence; consistency across turns matters, not just final-answer quality.\n</updated_cheatsheet>"
  ],
